    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>[H2]Adult Changes</title>
      <para id="para-00006">
        In middle age adults begin to notice changes in the body. 
Over time a transition occurs with the body experiencing decreasing muscle to increasing body fat. Age related muscle loss is termed as sarcopenia. The metabolism is how our bodies convert food to energy and includes components of energy consumption. Around the age of 46 the basic metabolic rate declines meaning the energy needed at rest is reduced, as seen in <link target-id="Figure04_03_SleepCycle"/> (Pontzer, et al., 2021).
<figure id="Figure04_03_SleepCycle">
  <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure04_03_SleepCycle.png"/>
  </media>
    <caption>The sleep cycle has four stages.</caption>
</figure>
Skin provides noticeable signs of aging such as wrinkles, age spots, and sagging. The layers of skin lose their elasticity, water and fat content causing the loose and wrinkled changes to the skin. The sun causes a major impact of secondary aging to the skin resulting in <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00001">sun spots</term>, in addition to wrinkling.
      </para>
      <para id="para-00007">
        One substantial concern in middle adulthood is the changing of <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00002">bone density</term>. In the mid to late 30s there the body reaches a peak in bone density, and then bone mass progressively declines. A process called osteoporosis. For women, <term id="term-00003">osteoporosis</term> occurs at twice the rate than it does for men as a result of menopause and the reduction of estrogen and progesterone. Hormone replacement therapy has been observed to diminish the impact of bone loss in menopause (Kaur, 2012). The risk of osteoporosis can be reduced by calcium consumption in <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00004">early adulthood</term>. The impacts can also be mitigated in adulthood with strength-training programs which have even shown bone density increases in women (Nelson et al., 1994).
      </para>
      <para id="para-00008">
        Unfortunately, the perception of negative stereotypes around aging also increases as adults move from middle or older age statuses. They generally used the methods in this bulleted list.
        <list id="list-00002">
          <item>Here is the first bulleted item.</item> 
          <item>Here is the second bulleted item. It i much longer so we can see what happens when items in the list need to wrap onto a second or even a third or even a fourth line.</item>
          <item>Here is the third bulleted item in the list.</item>
        </list>
        Now the list is complete.
      </para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00007">
      <title>[H2]Brain Changes in Middle Adulthood</title>
      <para id="para-00009">
        What happens to our brain now that it is fully formed? Just because the growing process has stopped does not mean the brain has ceased developing too. There is a great deal of stability in brain during middle adulthood and aspects that are indistinguishable from that of early adult such as the ability to make and utilize mental images (Cacola, et al., 2013). Researchers have identified differences in other areas though, some of which show improved functioning and some of which show less functioning, as shown in <link target-id="Figure02_05_BrownBoard"/>.
      </para>
        <figure id="Figure02_05_BrownBoard">
          <media alt="Part a shows the seal of the Massachusetts Bay Colony. Part b shows a portrait of John Winthrop.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure02_05_BrownBoard.png"/>
          </media>
          <caption>(a) In the 1629 seal of the Massachusetts Bay Colony, an Indian is shown asking colonists to “Come over and help us.” This seal indicates the religious ambitions of (b) John Winthrop, the colony’s first governor, for his “city upon a hill.”</caption>
        </figure>
      <para id="para-00010">
        The <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00005">brain</term> reflects overall more stable emotions in middle adulthood. Findings suggest middle age adults are better at focusing on positive information and less amygdala reactivity to negative information as compared to younger adults (Maher et al., 2004). It is not uncommon to hear adults in middle age comment about becoming forgetful and even presenting for memory assessments in middle age. This is not consistent with research and often times the perceived problems in memory are a result of stress and anxiety. In fact, differences in memory between middle and young adults are not typically found and seem to generally function at the same level (Salthouse, 2012).
      </para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00008">
      <title>[H2]This Section Will Show Four of the Feature Boxes in <emphasis effect="italics">Lifespan Development</emphasis></title>
      <para id="para-00011">
        One more commonly apparent change in brain processes in middle adulthood is a slower <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00006">processing speed</term> than of previous ages. This may be due to the middle-age adult using greater areas of the cortex in response to cognitive tasks than younger adults (Gunter et al., 1998), however chronic health problems are also associated with reduced processing speed (Tufvesson et al., 2013). Another reason for slowed processing speed maybe changes in the inhibition ability of middle-aged adults. Research has found adults to have more difficulty filtering distracting stimuli such as being interrupted by irrelevant verbal stimuli on a task (Kray et al., 2008).
      </para>
      <note class="what-heard" id="note-00001">
        <title>Sample Feature Box Using Lorem Ipsum Bob Ross</title>
        <para id="para-00012">
