    <section id="sect-00009">
      <title>[H2]Sensation and Perception in Middle Adulthood </title>
      <para id="para-00026">
        Just as people experience changes to their brain and body, sensory abilities also begin to experience changes during middle adulthood. Structural components of the eyes change. Hearing acuity recedes from primary and secondary aging. Even other senses experience subtle changes during this time. As seen in <link target-id="Figure02_05_variables"/>, the eyes experience a loss of visual acuity in middle age referred to as <term id="term-00011">presbyopia</term>. This results from a thickening of the lens which reduces the amount of light that can reach the retina. Two common issues arise from presbyopia. As the thickening occurs it reduces the elasticity of the eye causing it to become more farsighted. A reason many adults rely on reading glass and bifocals to increase visual acuity when reading. Additionally, a yellow material builds up from the thickened lens reducing light. The limitation of light influences the comfort of night driving. Drivers experiencing presbyopia may have difficulty reading street signs at night or become more disrupted by the headlights of other cars. Night driving is additionally complicated by a significant loss of rods in middle adulthood (Grossniklaus et al., 2013). A further issue with lens thickening is that it puts more strain on the on the muscles around the eye making it more difficult to focus on objects.
      </para>
        <figure id="Figure02_05_variables">
          <media alt="A map shows the “Columbian Exchange” of goods and diseases. Goods include crops such as maize, potatoes, tobacco, beans, squash, peppers, cacao, cassava, and manioc traveling east as well as rye, wheat, rice, sugar, and tea traveling west. Animals such as cattle, horses, and pigs traveled westward. Diseases include syphilis, malaria, smallpox, yellow fever, and plague.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure02_05_variables.png"/>
          </media>
          <caption>With European exploration and settlement of the New World, goods, animals, and diseases began crossing the Atlantic Ocean in both directions. This “Columbian Exchange” soon had global implications. (attribution: Copyright Rice University, OpenStax, under CC BY 4.0 license)</caption>
        </figure>
      <section id="sect-00010">
        <title>[H3]Presbycusis</title>
        <para id="para-00027">
          Age related hearing loss is known as <term id="term-00012">presbycusis</term>. Statistics vary widely on the percent of middle-aged adults with hearing loss, however it is safe to say it begins to affect many during this time. Over time the inner ear’s structures and nerves slowly deteriorate. Initial hearing loss begins at the extremities with high frequency sounds. Secondary causes of hearing loss such as noise exposure are also common. Men tend to experience more <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00013">hearing loss</term> than women do which maybe an aspect of working in professions or hobbies with more noise exposure. For example, the second most common disability benefit to US service members is hearing loss with 1.3 million receiving compensation (Veteran Benefit Administration, 2020). This is only topped by tinnitus, another hearing related condition. Hearing aids can be beneficial to those struggling with hearing loss. However, many avoid or put off compensating their hearing due to hassle or cost (Li-Korotky, 2012). Those who do choose hearing aids may find that it reduces rates of cognitive declines and even dementia risk (Yeo et al., 2022). 
        </para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00011">
        <title>[H3]Middle Age</title>
        <para id="para-00028">
          Some research has suggested other sensory capabilities may also be different in middle age. As skin thins and loses elasticity, the touch sensation and sensitivity to hot and cold become more pronounced. Proprioception, our body’s sense of movement in space has been observed to not be as skilled at rebalancing itself at middle age (45-64) as compared to earlier adulthood (23-34; Pinto et al., 2020).
        </para>
      </section>
    </section>
